# tools/conformance/css_mixins_suite.py: the templates of web-platform-tests' css-mixins pages that fail and whose expectation does not bind this library, one line a case. Columns (tab separated): vector
# (file#n), what it asks, stage (value, run), the answer the library gives (`actual=<our --actual> | expected=<our --expected>`), class, and the reason, which quotes the sentence of the served draft
# (css-mixins-1, as `css-mixins-1: "the sentence"`) that decides it, and says what Chromium 156.0.8078.47 and Firefox 158.0.1 answer where the style differential asked them (the `functions` scope of
# tools/oracle/css_style_diff.py asks both of every template of these pages). A case that passes, that is not here, whose answer is not the one listed, or whose reason quotes nothing, or a sentence the draft
# does not say, fails the gate. Classes:
#   draft-newer        the served draft says what the page says and the pinned data predates it
#   wpt-tentative      the page is a tentative test: no specification decides it yet
#   needs-environment  the answer depends on the document, the layout or the user agent, which the runner does not have
#   wpt-wrong          the page contradicts the served draft
css/css-mixins/function-parameter-scoping.html#1	a default that references a later parameter (`--a: var(--b)`, `--b: 3px`) is guaranteed-invalid, so the call is -1px	value	actual=3px | expected=-1px	wpt-wrong	css-mixins-1: "Add a custom property to argument rule with a name of the parameter's name, and a value of first-valid(arg value, default value)." The argument rule is one style rule that holds a custom property for every parameter, so `var(--b)` in the default of `--a` is read like any `var()` of a custom property of one rule, and sees `--b`: 3px. The page's own comment says a default 'can reference an earlier parameter but never sees ... a later one', which no sentence of the draft says. Chromium 156.0.8078.47 answers 3px as this library does; Firefox 158.0.1 implements no @function (the style differential, functions scope)
css/css-mixins/function-parameter-scoping.html#2	a default that references a later parameter does not see the calling element's custom property of that name	value	actual=3px | expected=-1px	wpt-wrong	css-mixins-1: "Add a custom property to argument rule with a name of the parameter's name, and a value of first-valid(arg value, default value)." The later parameter `--b` is a property of the argument rule and shadows the element's `--b: 99px` (3px is its default), which is not what the page expects (-1px). Chromium 156.0.8078.47 answers 3px as this library does; Firefox 158.0.1 implements no @function
css/css-mixins/functions/dashed-function-eval.html#59	`@function --f(--x) { result: 10px }` called as `--f()`: a parameter with no default and no argument makes the function guaranteed-invalid	value	actual=10px | expected=	wpt-wrong	css-mixins-1: "Let arg value be the value of the corresponding argument in arguments, or the guaranteed-invalid value if there is no corresponding argument." The missing argument is the value of the parameter `--x`, which this body does not read: step 14 returns the value of `result`, 10px. No sentence makes a missing argument without a default the guaranteed-invalid value of the call (the older text did). Chromium 156.0.8078.47 answers the guaranteed-invalid value, the page's expectation; Firefox 158.0.1 implements no @function
css/css-mixins/functions/dashed-function-eval.html#60	`--f(1, 2)` of `--f(--x, --y, --z)`: a missing argument makes the function guaranteed-invalid	value	actual=10px | expected=	wpt-wrong	css-mixins-1: "Let arg value be the value of the corresponding argument in arguments, or the guaranteed-invalid value if there is no corresponding argument." `--z` is the guaranteed-invalid value and the body (`result: 10px`) does not read it, so the call is 10px. Chromium 156.0.8078.47 answers the guaranteed-invalid value, the page's expectation; Firefox 158.0.1 implements no @function
